HalluSpot — Judging Unseen Generators Against Counterfactual Contexts An LLM was given a prompt with source material — web passages, a news article, or a business record — and wrote an answer. For every sentence of every answer, predict the probability that it is hallucinated: not supported by, or contradicting, the prompt as given here. Three things make this harder than standard hallucination detection: Unseen generators. Training answers come from GPT-3.5, GPT-4 and Llama-2-7B/13B. Test answers come only from two models never seen in training: Mistral-7B (an unseen model family) and Llama-2-70B (an unseen model size). Their errors differ from the ones you train on. Counterfactual contexts. Some prompts were edited after the answers were written. A number the answer states may have been changed or swapped in the prompt, which makes the sentence a contradiction even though it matched the original source. A claim the answer invented may now be stated in the prompt, which makes it supported. The prompt in sources.csv is the only reference — not the original document and not world knowledge. Detectors trained on the original RAGTruth labels do not transfer: copying those labels scores −0.34, below the anchor. No world-knowledge shortcut. Names and numbers are pseudonymised consistently within each prompt and its answers, so a sentence can only be checked against the prompt. Data task_type: QA (answer a question from three passages), Summary (summarise a news article), Data2txt (describe a business from its JSON record and reviews). position: where the sentence sits in its answer, from 0 (first) to 1 (last). Sort by it within a response_id to rebuild the answer. p_hallucinated in train.csv is 1 if the sentence is hallucinated with respect to its prompt, else 0. About 15 % of training sentences are positive. Training data contains edited prompts too. Derived from RAGTruth (MIT licence; human-annotated hallucination spans). The split is by prompt: no prompt, answer or source passage is shared between train and test. Submission One row per test sentence (6,198 rows), in any order: sentence_id,p_hallucinated s_1b388830134803,0.07 A submission with a missing, duplicated or unknown sentence_id, a missing column, or a wrong row count is rejected. Values are clipped to [0, 1]; non-numeric values count as 0. Metric Score = 0.5 · skill(AUC_sentence) + 0.3 · skill(AUC_response) + 0.2 · skill(Brier) AUC_sentence — ROC AUC over all test sentences. AUC_response — ROC AUC over test answers. An answer's score is the maximum over its sentences, and the answer is positive if any of its sentences is. Brier — mean squared error of p_hallucinated. Each term is normalised so the anchor scores 0 and a perfect submission 1: u = (AUC − anchor) / (1 − anchor) for the AUCs and u = 1 − Brier / anchor_Brier for Brier. Then skill(u) = u for u ≥ 0 and u / (1 − u) below, so scores lie in (−1, 1]. import numpy as np, pandas as pd SENT_ANCH, RESP_ANCH, D_BRIER = 0.7427490333370979, 0.8339023502718438, 0.07449352079989884 def skill(u): return min(u, 1.0) if u >= 0 else u / (1.0 - u) def auc(y, s): # tie-aware ROC AUC y = y.astype(bool); npos, nneg = y.sum(), (~y).sum() if npos == 0 or nneg == 0: return 0.5 o = np.argsort(s, kind="mergesort"); sv = s[o]; r = np.empty(len(s)) st = np.r_[0, np.flatnonzero(np.diff(sv) != 0) + 1, len(sv)] for a, b in zip(st[:-1], st[1:]): r[o[a:b]] = 0.5 * (a + b - 1) + 1.0 return float((r[y].sum() - npos * (npos + 1) / 2.0) / (npos * nneg)) def score(sub, ans): # ans: sentence_id, p_hallucinated (a train.csv slice works) ans = ans.drop_duplicates("sentence_id").set_index("sentence_id") sub = sub.drop_duplicates("sentence_id").set_index("sentence_id").loc[ans.index] p = pd.to_numeric(sub["p_hallucinated"], errors="coerce").to_numpy(float) p = np.clip(np.where(np.isfinite(p), p, 0.0), 0.0, 1.0) y = ans["p_hallucinated"].to_numpy(int) resp = pd.DataFrame({"r": [i[2:10] for i in ans.index], "p": p, "y": y}).groupby("r").max() s1 = skill((auc(y, p) - SENT_ANCH) / (1 - SENT_ANCH)) s2 = skill((auc(resp["y"].to_numpy(), resp["p"].to_numpy()) - RESP_ANCH) / (1 - RESP_ANCH)) s3 = skill(1.0 - ((p - y) ** 2).mean() / D_BRIER) return round(0.5 * s1 + 0.3 * s2 + 0.2 * s3, 12) This is the leaderboard metric; the first 8 hex digits of a sentence_id are its response_id. Validate by holding out whole prompts, ideally also whole generators — the test generators are not in train.csv. Baselines Rules No RAGTruth. The public corpus, anything derived from it, and models trained on it are forbidden. General-purpose public models (LLMs, NLI, cross-encoders) are allowed. Ids and row order are not features; use source_id / response_id only to join. Fix all random seeds. No environment-variable switches, no time-based truncation, no remote inference. no network at run time except cached model weights. &nbsp;
Closes in 3h 44m
